> # Tallybook
>
> I keep the books for my household and a small side business, and I have outgrown spreadsheets. I want a proper
> double-entry bookkeeping tool that I can script, run from the terminal and put behind a small web service for the
> family. I'm calling it **tallybook**. Build it carefully rather than quickly; the pieces depend on each other, so read
> the whole document first.
>
> It comes in three layers that share one engine:
>
> - a **library**, `import tallybook`, which does all the real work: money, currencies, dates, accounts, a ledger, a
>   text journal, queries, rules, CSV import, budgets, recurring entries, reports and export;
> - a **database** (SQLite, standard library) with numbered schema versions and migrations, so a book I start today
>   still opens after I update the tool;
> - a **command line**, `tallybook`, and a small **HTTP API**, `tallybook serve`, both thin layers on the library.
>
> Python 3.9 or newer, standard library only (no third-party packages, no network at build or run time). Running
> `pip install .` in the project folder must give me both the importable `tallybook` package and the `tallybook`
> command (`python -m tallybook` should do the same thing). How you lay out the files is up to you, but the module,
> class and function names I give below are what my own scripts import, so use exactly those. Everything is
> deterministic: nothing reads the clock except where I say "today", and wherever "today" matters I can pass the date in.
>
> *(continues in `brief.md`, sections: How to read this document · Money · Currencies and exchange rates · Dates and periods · Accounts · The ledger · The journal file · Finding transactions: the query language · Rules · Importing my bank's CSV files · Budgets · Recurring transactions · Reports · Export, and the plain text tables · The book file: `tallybook.storage` · The command line · The web API)*

## Scope change 1 (user request, verbatim)

> # A change to what I asked for
>
> I have been using the first version of this and I want three things changed. Everything else stays as I described it.
>
> ## 1. Round half to even
>
> Wherever you round a result to a currency's smallest unit (or to the tenth of a percent), round half to even (banker's rounding) instead of half up: when the value is exactly halfway, the result is the neighbouring even number. 0.5 becomes 0, 1.5 becomes 2, 2.5 becomes 2, -2.5 becomes -2; a percentage of 0.25 becomes 0.2 and 0.35 becomes 0.4. This is `round_div` and so everything that uses it: converting an amount between currencies (`RateTable.convert`, the converted reports, `calc money convert`), multiplying an amount by a ratio (`Money.scale`, `calc money scale`), the part of a budget that falls in a period (the prorated allowance), the "percent used" of a budget (one decimal), and the percentages in the reports. Dividing an amount among parts (`allocate`, largest remainder) does not change.
>
> ## 2. A lock date
>
> I want to close a period so that nobody, me included, can change it by accident. The book can have a lock date. A transaction dated on or before the lock date can no longer be added, deleted, imported, or created by the recurring entries. The operation is refused with the error code `locked` and **nothing at all is changed**: an import that contains one locked row adds none of its rows, a journal import adds none of its accounts, rates or transactions, and a refused `materialize` does not move any series forward. Nothing else is affected (accounts, rates, budgets, rules, reports, closing an account).
>
> - **Library.** `tallybook.errors.LockedPeriodError` is a kind of `ConflictError` with the code `locked`; its message contains the word `locked`. `Store.set_lock(day)` sets the lock to a date (it may be moved earlier or later), `Store.set_lock(None)` removes it, `Store.lock_date()` returns the date or `None`; the lock is kept in the book file. `Ledger` has an attribute `lock_date` (`None` by default; I may assign it) that is honoured by `Ledger.add`, `Ledger.add_many` (all or nothing), `Ledger.remove` (by the date of the transaction removed) and `Ledger.replace` (refused if the old or the new date is locked). `Store.ledger()` returns a ledger whose `lock_date` is the store's. `Store.add_transaction`, `add_transactions`, `delete_transaction`, `import_csv`, `load_journal` and `materialize` raise `LockedPeriodError` for a locked date.
> - **Command line.** `tallybook lock show` prints the lock date as `YYYY-MM-DD`, or `none`; `tallybook lock set 2024-03-31` prints `locked through 2024-03-31` and `tallybook lock clear` prints `lock cleared` (also when there is no lock); all exit 0. A malformed date is exit 1; a missing or surplus argument, or `lock` alone, exit 2. Any refused operation (`tx add`, `tx delete`, `journal import`, `import`, `recurring materialize`) is exit 1 with the usual `tallybook: <message>` on standard error and nothing on standard output. Before this change `lock` is an unknown command (exit 2).
> - **HTTP API.** `GET /lock` answers 200 `{"lock_date": "2024-03-31"}`, or `{"lock_date": null}` when there is no lock. `PUT /lock` with the same body sets it, and `{"lock_date": null}` removes it; it answers 200 with the same shape showing the lock now in force. The field `lock_date` must be present (`{}` is an error) and nothing else may be sent; a date that is not `YYYY-MM-DD` is a parse error and a value that is neither text nor `null` a validation error (statuses below). `POST` or `DELETE` on `/lock` is 405 with `Allow: GET, PUT`. A refused operation (`POST /transactions`, `DELETE /transactions/<id>`, `POST /import`, `POST /recurring/materialize`) answers 409 with the code `locked`. Before this change `/lock` is a 404 like any unknown path.
>
> ## 3. The API's error body
>
> An error answer from the HTTP API has the body `{"error": {"code": "<code>", "message": "<text>"}}` instead of `{"error": "<text>"}`. The `code` is the error's code (`parse`, `validation`, `not_found`, `conflict`, `locked`), which includes an unknown path (`not_found`); for a request the server turns away before it gets to any of that it is `bad_request` (an unreadable request line or a `Content-Length` that is not a number: status 400), `method_not_allowed` (405), `unsupported_media_type` (415) or `too_large` (413); a failure of the server's own is `internal` (500). Errors in what I sent (invalid JSON, a value that does not parse or fails a rule: codes `parse` and `validation`) answer **422** instead of 400. Every other status stays as it is.

"""Error hierarchy: every user-caused failure derives from TallybookError."""


class TallybookError(Exception):
    code = "error"

    def __init__(self, message="", **extra):
        super().__init__(message)
        self.message = message
        self.detail = {}
        for key, value in extra.items():
            setattr(self, key, value)

    def __str__(self):
        return self.message


class ParseError(TallybookError):
    code = "parse"


class ValidationError(TallybookError):
    code = "validation"


class NotFoundError(TallybookError):
    code = "not_found"


class ConflictError(TallybookError):
    code = "conflict"


class _Raw:
    """Shows a text exactly as given (inside quotes) in an error message, control characters included."""

    __slots__ = ("value",)

    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value

    def __repr__(self):
        if isinstance(self.value, str):
            return "'" + self.value + "'"
        return repr(self.value)

    def __str__(self):
        return str(self.value)


def raw(value):
    return _Raw(value)
"""The ledger: validated transactions and balances."""
import re

from tallybook.accounts import Chart
from tallybook.currency import RateTable
from tallybook.dates import format_date, is_date
from tallybook.errors import NotFoundError, ValidationError, raw
from tallybook.model import Posting, Transaction
from tallybook.money import Money, format_minor


class LedgerError(ValidationError):
    pass


_TAG = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_-]*")


def _is_int(value):
    return isinstance(value, int) and not isinstance(value, bool)


def _line_free(text):
    return isinstance(text, str) and "\n" not in text and "\r" not in text


def _copy(tx):
    return Transaction(tx.date, tx.payee, tx.narration, list(tx.postings), tuple(tx.tags), tx.flag, tx.id)


class Ledger:
    def __init__(self, chart=None, rates=None):
        self.chart = chart if chart is not None else Chart()
        self.rates = rates if rates is not None else RateTable()
        self._txs = {}
        self._max_id = 0

    def __len__(self):
        return len(self._txs)

    # -- validation ---------------------------------------------------
    def _build(self, tx, forced_id=None, check_id=True):
        if not isinstance(tx, Transaction):
            raise LedgerError("expected a Transaction, got %r" % (raw(tx),))
        if not is_date(tx.date):
            raise LedgerError("date must be a datetime.date, got %r" % (raw(tx.date),))
        if tx.flag not in ("*", "!") or not isinstance(tx.flag, str):
            raise LedgerError("flag must be '*' or '!', got %r" % (raw(tx.flag),))
        if not _line_free(tx.payee):
            raise LedgerError("payee must be text without a line break")
        if not _line_free(tx.narration):
            raise LedgerError("narration must be text without a line break")
        if isinstance(tx.tags, str):
            raise LedgerError("tags must be a sequence of tag names")
        try:
            tags = list(tx.tags)
        except TypeError:
            raise LedgerError("tags must be a sequence of tag names") from None
        for tag in tags:
            if not isinstance(tag, str) or _TAG.fullmatch(tag) is None:
                raise LedgerError("invalid tag %r" % (raw(tag),))
        try:
            postings = list(tx.postings)
        except TypeError:
            raise LedgerError("postings must be a list") from None
        for p in postings:
            if not isinstance(p, Posting):
                raise LedgerError("expected a Posting, got %r" % (raw(p),))
            if not isinstance(p.account, str):
                raise LedgerError("posting account must be text, got %r" % (raw(p.account),))
            if not _line_free(p.note):
                raise LedgerError("posting note must be text without a line break")
            if p.amount is not None and not isinstance(p.amount, Money):
                raise LedgerError("posting amount must be Money or None, got %r" % (raw(p.amount),))
        if len(postings) < 2:
            raise LedgerError("a transaction needs at least two postings")
        for p in postings:
            if not self.chart.exists(p.account):
                raise LedgerError("unknown account %s" % p.account)
        for p in postings:
            if not self.chart.is_open(p.account, tx.date):
                raise LedgerError("account %s is not open on %s" % (p.account, format_date(tx.date)))
        self._check_currencies(postings)
        missing = [i for i, p in enumerate(postings) if p.amount is None]
        if len(missing) > 1:
            raise LedgerError("more than one posting without an amount")
        sums = {}
        for p in postings:
            if p.amount is not None:
                sums[p.amount.currency] = sums.get(p.amount.currency, 0) + p.amount.minor
        off = {c: v for c, v in sums.items() if v != 0}
        if missing:
            if not off:
                raise LedgerError("nothing to infer: the other postings already balance")
            i = missing[0]
            gap = postings[i]
            filled = [Posting(gap.account, Money(-off[c], c), gap.note) for c in sorted(off)]
            postings = postings[:i] + filled + postings[i + 1 :]
            self._check_currencies(filled)
        elif off:
            detail = ", ".join("%s %s" % (c, format_minor(off[c], c)) for c in sorted(off))
            raise LedgerError("transaction does not balance: %s" % detail)
        if forced_id is not None:
            tid = forced_id
        elif tx.id is None:
            tid = self._max_id + 1
        else:
            if not _is_int(tx.id) or tx.id < 1:
                raise LedgerError("id must be an integer of at least 1, got %r" % (raw(tx.id),))
            if check_id and tx.id in self._txs:
                raise LedgerError("transaction id %d already exists" % tx.id)
            tid = tx.id
        return Transaction(tx.date, tx.payee, tx.narration, postings, tuple(sorted(set(tags))), tx.flag, tid)

    def _check_currencies(self, postings):
        for p in postings:
            if p.amount is None:
                continue
            held = self.chart.get(p.account).currency
            if held is not None and held != p.amount.currency:
                raise LedgerError("account %s only holds %s, not %s" % (p.account, held, p.amount.currency))

    def _store(self, stored):
        self._txs[stored.id] = stored
        if stored.id > self._max_id:
            self._max_id = stored.id

    # -- writes -------------------------------------------------------
    def add(self, tx):
        stored = self._build(tx)
        self._store(stored)
        return _copy(stored)

    def add_many(self, transactions):
        saved_txs, saved_max = dict(self._txs), self._max_id
        out = []
        try:
            for tx in transactions:
                out.append(self.add(tx))
        except BaseException:
            self._txs, self._max_id = saved_txs, saved_max
            raise
        return out

    def replace(self, id, tx):
        if id not in self._txs:
            raise NotFoundError("no transaction %s" % (id,))
        if isinstance(tx, Transaction) and tx.id is not None and tx.id != id:
            raise LedgerError("replacement id %r does not match %r" % (raw(tx.id), raw(id)))
        stored = self._build(tx, forced_id=id)
        self._txs[id] = stored
        return _copy(stored)

    def remove(self, id):
        if id not in self._txs:
            raise NotFoundError("no transaction %s" % (id,))
        return _copy(self._txs.pop(id))

    # -- reads --------------------------------------------------------
    def get(self, id):
        if id not in self._txs:
            raise NotFoundError("no transaction %s" % (id,))
        return _copy(self._txs[id])

    def _sorted(self):
        return sorted(self._txs.values(), key=lambda t: (t.date, t.id))

    def transactions(self):
        return [_copy(t) for t in self._sorted()]

    def postings(self):
        for tx in self._sorted():
            copy = _copy(tx)
            for p in copy.postings:
                yield copy, p

    def _selected(self, as_of):
        if as_of is not None and not is_date(as_of):
            raise LedgerError("as_of must be a date or None, got %r" % (raw(as_of),))
        for tx in self._txs.values():
            if as_of is None or tx.date <= as_of:
                yield tx

    def balances(self, as_of=None):
        totals = {}
        for tx in self._selected(as_of):
            for p in tx.postings:
                cur = totals.setdefault(p.account, {})
                cur[p.amount.currency] = cur.get(p.amount.currency, 0) + p.amount.minor
        return _clean(totals)

    def rollup_balances(self, as_of=None):
        totals = {}
        for tx in self._selected(as_of):
            for p in tx.postings:
                path = p.account
                while True:
                    cur = totals.setdefault(path, {})
                    cur[p.amount.currency] = cur.get(p.amount.currency, 0) + p.amount.minor
                    if ":" not in path:
                        break
                    path = path.rsplit(":", 1)[0]
        return _clean(totals)

    def balance(self, account, as_of=None, currency=None, rollup=True):
        if not self.chart.exists(account):
            raise NotFoundError("no such account: %s" % (account,))
        prefix = account + ":"
        sums = {}
        for tx in self._selected(as_of):
            for p in tx.postings:
                if p.account == account or (rollup and p.account.startswith(prefix)):
                    c = p.amount.currency
                    sums[c] = sums.get(c, 0) + p.amount.minor
        if currency is not None:
            return Money(sums.get(currency, 0), currency)
        return {c: Money(v, c) for c, v in sorted(sums.items()) if v != 0}


def _clean(totals):
    out = {}
    for account, cur in totals.items():
        kept = {c: v for c, v in cur.items() if v != 0}
        if kept:
            out[account] = kept
    return out

